  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nH850 southerly winds around 20 to 25KT continue to direct mass \nand high moisture into an effective frontal zone draped across \nnorthern counties through mid-morning. Precipitable water values\nremain high near 1.8 to 1.9 inches. Rain totals between 1 and 3\ninches have already fallen overnight on top of heavy rainfall \nfrom the previous night, leaving soils saturated and highly \nvulnerable to additional runoff. The existing Flash Flood Watch \ncontinues through 11 AM EDT this morning, as additional \ndownpours could rapidly trigger localized flash flooding. The \nbulk of this morning activity is forecast to diminish and exit \nto the east late this morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nConsiderable uncertainty remains regarding the exact placement\nof an outflow-reinforced frontal boundary late this afternoon\ninto this evening. Recent model trends suggest the boundary may\npark farther north than previously indicated, potentially\nsettling near the Metro Valley. This boundary will serve as the\nprimary focus for renewed convective initiation late this \nafternoon and evening. Moderate surface heating ahead of the \nboundary will drive MLCAPE values between 1500 and 2500J/kg. \nDeep-layer 0-6 km shear is modest around 25 to 30KTs supporting\nsome storm organization with steep low-level lapse rates and \nDCAPE values exceeding 500 J/kg will support wet downburst \npotential. Given full leaf canopy and very wet antecedent soils \nacross Appalachia, local wind damage thresholds are reduced, \nshallow rooted trees easily toppling in sub severe winds.\nCurrently the SPC depicts a marginal risk of severe weather\nacross the southern half of the area with mainly a damaging wind\nthreat expected. With some modest low level turning in the\nvicinity of the boundary and very moist low levels, could not\nalso rule out a tornado.\n\nHigh rainfall rates exceeding 1 to 2 inches per hour will pose \na flash flood threat over any areas that saw previous heavy\nrainfall. If the farther north boundary position verifies, an \nextension or a new Flash Flood Watch would likely be necessary \nlate this afternoon and overnight for sensitive soils just north\nof the Metro Valley. A farther south solution would allow \nrainfall to hit soils more capable of handling a quick hit of \nprecipitation.\n\nWill continue to evaluate guidance this morning regarding\nmodifications to the Flash Flood Watch, but this decision may\nfall to the day shift.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe persistent ring of fire pattern breaks down midweek as an\nupper-level closed low deepens over the Ohio Valley and pushes\nthe polar front toward the Deep South. Temperatures will turn\nnoticeably cooler Wednesday and Thursday, with daytime highs in\nthe upper 50s to upper 60s, which is roughly 10 to 15 degrees\nbelow late September climatology. However, deep moisture will\nlinger in the atmospheric column while an inverted trough\nestablishes west of the mountains ahead of a developing coastal\nlow through Wednesday. Embedded shortwave disturbances in \nwesterly flow aloft combined will support additional slow-\nmoving showers and thunderstorms Tuesday into Wednesday. Given \nprevious rainfall and compromised soils, additional localized \nflash flooding will remain possible. As the coastal low tracks \nnorth toward the Middle Atlantic coast, low- level flow will \nback to the northeast, finally ushering in drier, stable air by \nlate in the week.",
